    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="2" relname="circumstance">In cases where the design is fit for the latest browser,</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="14" relname="span">many sites tend to leave the outdated users.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="15" relname="span">Many sites would recommend specific browsers</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="3" relname="volitional-result">and for those who choose not to, normally have to leave the site.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="16" relname="span">There are still users who still use AOL 3, NS 4, and other older browsers.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="11" relname="span">Good design and rules does not have to use the latest technology.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="17" relname="span">I have seen many developed pages using the latest technology (CSS, Flash, Scripts, etc.) to meet the current market trends and even more so on government websites that are focued for the general public including elderly.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="9" relname="antithesis">Many critics believe that the government websites should be using the latest technology.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="12" relname="span">However, they fail realize that many of users utilize these sites for information only.</segment>
        <group id="10" type="span"/>
        <group id="11" type="span" parent="10" relname="span"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="7" relname="nonvolitional-result"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="6" relname="antithesis"/>
        <group id="14" type="span" parent="13" relname="span"/>
        <group id="15" type="span" parent="5" relname="antithesis"/>
        <group id="16" type="span" parent="14" relname="evidence"/>
